"""record_25nov_li1_results_and_inflation.py -- TRACK 3.119: the results of test LI-1 and of the short-sale risk measurement (priors #214-#217 scored);
test LI-1b (daily stand-ins for core PCE) registered BEFORE it runs. Adds the sources to docs/SOURCES.md. Idempotent."""
from pathlib import Path
t = Path("docs/TRACK.md"); s = t.read_text()
A = """

### 3.119 LI-1 and the short-sale risk: results; test LI-1b registered: daily stand-ins for core PCE (25 Nov 23:55)
**LI-1, result (commit 26a2cc3; `docs/leading_indicator.md`).** 14 candidates x 2 look-backs x 2 look-aheads = 56 tests, limit: chance under 0.00089. **2 of 14 pass, both only at one day back and one day ahead:** high-yield bonds against Treasuries (HYG/IEF), link -0.069 (chance 0.0002); VIX, link +0.061 (chance 0.0002). **The sign is the opposite of 'credit leads the fall':** on the day after credit got worse or fear rose, the semiconductor fund on average went slightly UP -- a small next-day rebound. A link of 0.07 explains about half of one per cent of the fund's moves. Bank loans (BKLN) -0.063 (chance 0.0012) just misses the limit, same sign. The 12 others: no (all recorded in the file, including the nulls).
**The other way round (chip shares first) is the stronger link for:** private-credit lenders (BIZD/SPY) +0.138 against +0.047, VIX at 5 days +0.131 against +0.050, bank loans at 5 days +0.090 against -0.047. Semiconductor shares move first, lenders and loans follow. For HYG/IEF it is not (-0.008).
**By market condition (description, not tested against the limit).** The links are strongest in 'Low long-term rates, low or negative real rates': HYG/IEF -0.168, bank loans -0.174, LQD/IEF -0.114, VIX +0.116. HYG/IEF also -0.116 in 'A flat or inverted curve, a strong dollar'. In 'A steep curve, low short-term rates' and 'Rapid money growth, a high policy rate' the HYG/IEF link is -0.028 in both (nothing).
**Limits of the data.** The four FRED credit-spread series cover about three years only (0, 8 and 93-97 days in three of the four conditions): they could not be tested fairly, and say nothing either way. The rate on reserves starts in 2021. Single-name credit default swap spreads of AI borrowers are sold data and are not in the test.
**What it means for the founder's question of 25 Nov.** Among the free daily markets, none was found that moves BEFORE semiconductor shares fall. The 'shadow leading indicator' is not HYG, LQD, bank loans, private-credit lenders, regional banks, VIX, MOVE, Oracle or the yield curve. What exists is a small next-day rebound after credit stress -- not registered as a trade; it would need its own test from the next open with the real cost.
**Priors.** #214 (no candidate leads at the limit): WRONG, two do -- but with the opposite sign of the idea. #215 (the other way round is stronger for most credit candidates): MIXED -- right for bank loans and private-credit lenders, wrong for high-yield and investment-grade bonds. #216 (closest in a tight-money condition): WRONG -- strongest in 'Low long-term rates, low or negative real rates'.
**Short-sale risk, result (`docs/selloff_short_risk.md`).** US$70,000 sold short on an account of US$70,000. SS-2a, 185 trades: worst rise inside a trade middle +1.4%, 99 in 100 under +15.5%, largest +19.8% (AMD 2016-01-20); largest loss of one trade +14.4% = US$10,046 (SNDK 2026-07-28); cushion never under 50%. SS-2b, 372 trades: largest rise +18.1%, largest loss US$6,654 (SCHW 2023-03-10); cushion never under 50%. SS-2c, 380 trades: largest rise +56.7% (MS 2008-09-17), largest loss of one trade +34.1% = US$23,876 (the same trade); cushion under 50% once and under 30% once -- that one trade. **Reading:** SS-2a and SS-2b never came near a margin call in the past; SS-2c had one trade that would have caused one. Shorting stays about US$15 a trade cheaper than the 2x fund (3.114), and the 2x fund cannot lose more than the money put in -- the founder's choice for SS-2c. The broker's own margin requirement for short sales is still not known (asked of the founder, open).
**Prior #217:** first half WRONG, second half RIGHT.

**Founder, 25 Nov 23:30, word for word:** 'also can add one more candidate which can perfectly reflect on core PCE index, because this core PCE does not give data every day, hence we should use one replacement index which gives out data everyday.'
**Checked first: nothing published daily IS core PCE.** Core PCE comes once a month from the Bureau of Economic Analysis. The Cleveland Fed publishes an 'inflation nowcast' each business day (about 10:00 New York time), but (a) its page offers no data file, and (b) its core PCE figure changes only on the days new CPI or PCE figures arrive -- it is flat on all other days. It is not a daily series and is not used. So 'perfectly' cannot be had; the honest stand-in is **the inflation the bond market expects**, which moves every day.
**Test LI-1b (`python -m src.leading inflation`), registered before it runs.** Four candidates: the 5-year and the 10-year breakeven inflation rate (FRED T5YIE, T10YIE; from 2003), expected inflation 5 to 10 years ahead (FRED T5YIFR), and the inflation-protected Treasury fund against ordinary Treasuries (TIP/IEF; from 2003, same-day price). Same target (SOXX), same method, same look-backs and look-aheads as LI-1: 4 x 2 x 2 = 16 tests, limit: chance under 0.0031 (0.05 / 16), 5,000 calendar-slid copies, the comparison against the S&P 500, the other way round, the four market conditions of the main page. FRED series are used one day late (published the next morning). Result file: `docs/leading_inflation.md`.
**Also printed (description): how well each stand-in follows core PCE.** Monthly average of the stand-in against core PCE inflation over 12 months (FRED PCEPILFE): the link of their levels and of their 3-month changes. Known weakness, stated before the run: breakevens are tied to the CPI, not the PCE, include food and energy, and move with the oil price.
**Priors.** #218: no stand-in passes the limit. #219: no stand-in follows core PCE closely -- the link of levels is under 0.6 for all four. #220: the link of 3-month changes is under 0.3 for all four (they follow oil, not core prices).
"""
if "### 3.119 LI-1 and the short-sale risk" not in s: t.write_text(s.rstrip("\n") + A); print("  TRACK.md: 3.119 added")
else: print("  TRACK.md: 3.119 already there -- skipped")
p = Path("docs/SOURCES.md"); x = p.read_text()
rows = [("Bank of Cleveland, Inflation Nowcasting", "| Federal Reserve Bank of Cleveland, Inflation Nowcasting (clevelandfed.org/indicators-and-data/inflation-nowcasting) | checked as a daily stand-in for core PCE (TRACK 3.119) -- NOT used | primary; page read 25 Nov 2026: no data file, and the core PCE figure changes only when new CPI or PCE data arrive |"),
        ("T5YIE, T10YIE, T5YIFR", "| FRED: T5YIE, T10YIE, T5YIFR (market-based expected inflation), PCEPILFE (core PCE price index, Bureau of Economic Analysis) | test LI-1b, daily stand-ins for core PCE (TRACK 3.119) | primary, free; breakevens follow the CPI, not the PCE |")]
for key, row in rows:
    if key in x: print(f"  SOURCES.md: '{key}' already there -- skipped")
    else: x = x.rstrip("\n") + "\n" + row + "\n"; print(f"  SOURCES.md: '{key}' added")
